From 02b91a765230df94320db12c83bcb6e8d5c89df1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AzediZ <145237384+AzediZ@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 14:23:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 099/110] Server stability and memory

Long sessions grew by a few MB an hour, some paths freed packets twice,
and several crashes showed up once the universe got busy.  The refcount
rules are now the same across the server:  containers take over the
reference passed to SetItem/AddItem, generated packet structs own their
pointer fields, SendNotification, QueueDestinyUpdate/Event, Multicast
and CorpNotify consume their payload (early returns included), and the
single destiny sends and PyTuple** bubblecasts don't.  Later patches
rely on these rules.

Fixes rolled in:
- packets nobody would receive aren't built, and object cache ids are
  freed
- a module whose first cycle killed its target kept a pointer to it and
  used the freed target at the end of the cycle;  a shutdown with ships
  or drones locked on dungeon NPCs crashed
- ships that leave a fight stop their modules first, and entities going
  while the server halts clear their locks until the world is actually
  being taken down (both read freed modules or targets before)
- a ship landing where two bubbles overlap joins the bubble with the
  entities in it instead of an empty one beside it
- a station loading before its system has booted no longer crashes
- a character loaded while its player was offline (show info) no
  longer writes its old wallet, location and corp back at shutdown or
  login
- goods put into a courier package or any cargo container no longer
  crash the server (the container's structure entity was never set)
- a podded pilot leaves its old bubble's player list
- indexes for reading a character's rows and for character names, and
  the server's login counter is an INT (it overflowed a SMALLINT)
